## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical mastery of low level synchronization, memory ordering, and reusability. A barrier is fundamental in frame sync, parallel tasks, and NUMA awareness, but many candidates overfit to simple mutex patterns.

## Key Concepts

- C11 atomics and memory ordering
- Sense-reversing barrier design
- Reuse safety and race avoidance
- Spin-wait costs and pause hints
- Microbenchmark methodology

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <sched.h>
volatile atomic_int arrivals;
volatile atomic_int generation;

void barrier(int N){
  int gen = atomic_load_explicit(&generation, memory_order_acquire);
  if (atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&arrivals, 1, memory_order_acq_rel) == N-1){
    atomic_store_explicit(&arrivals, 0, memory_order_release);
    atomic_store_explicit(&generation, 1 - gen, memory_order_release);
  } else {
    while (atomic_load_explicit(&generation, memory_order_acquire) == gen){
      sched_yield();
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for false sharing avoidance across cores?
- How would you measure livelock risk and scale beyond 16 threads?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design crash‑consistent data paths in persistent memory, a common production issue when power failures occur during in‑flight operations.

## Key Concepts

- Durability guarantees for PMEM writes
- Memory ordering: release/acquire, fences, cacheline flushes
- Recovery via redo log and slot sequence counters
- ABA avoidance in a concurrent ring
- Testing with fault injection and crash replay

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-code: enqueue/dequeue with PMEM flush/fence
// Slot: { data, seq, valid }
void enqueue(T v){
  size_t t = tail.fetch_add(1);
  Slot *s = &ring[t % CAP];
  s->data = v; // write data
  clwb(&s->data, sizeof(T));
  fence(); // ensure data persists
  s->seq = t; // publish with durable tail update (release)
  clwb(&s->seq, sizeof(size_t));
  fence();
  // publish tail already happens via atomic tail update
}

bool dequeue(T *out){
  size_t h0 = head.load();
  Slot *s = &ring[h0 % CAP];
  if (s->seq != h0) return false;
  *out = s->data;
  s->valid = false;
  clwb(&s->valid, sizeof(bool));
  fence();
  head.fetch_add(1);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle power loss during the publish vs data write steps?
- How would you validate recovery correctness across multiple restart scenarios?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests lock-free design, ABA avoidance with sequence counters, memory ordering, and NUMA-aware reclamation strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free queues with sequence counters
- Epoch-based memory reclamation
- NUMA locality and cache-line padding
- Memory order: acquire/release and fences
- Batch enqueues to reduce barrier overhead

## Code Example
```cpp
// Pseudo-enqueue (simplified)
struct Slot { uint64_t seq; char data[128]; };
Slot ring[N];
_Atomic uint64_t head=0, tail=0;
void enqueue(void* msg){
  uint64_t pos = atomic_fetch_add(&tail, 1);
  Slot& s = ring[pos & (N-1)];
  while (s.seq != pos) _mm_pause();
  memcpy(s.data, msg, 128);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s.seq, pos+1, memory_order_release);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to multiple consumers while preserving throughput?
- What are the trade-offs of epoch-based reclamation vs hazard pointers in this scenario?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of low-level concurrency, lock-free design under real contention.

## Key Concepts

- MPMC queue design with sequence numbers
- ABA protection via monotonic sequences
- Memory reclamation (hazard pointers, epochs)
- Cache friendliness and false sharing prevention

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-code
struct Slot {
    atomic_uint64_t seq;
    void* data;
};

bool enqueue(void* item) {
    uint64_t pos = tail.load();
    Slot* slot = &buffer[pos & mask];
    uint64_t seq = slot->seq.load();
    if ((seq & mask) != (pos & mask)) return false;
    if (!tail.compare_exchange(pos, pos + 1)) return false;
    slot->data = item;
    slot->seq.store(pos + 1);
    return true;
}

void* dequeue() {
    uint64_t pos = head.load();
    Slot* slot = &buffer[pos & mask];
    uint64_t seq = slot->seq.load();
    if ((seq & mask) != (pos & mask)) return nullptr;
    if (!head.compare_exchange(pos, pos + 1)) return nullptr;
    void* item = slot->data;
    slot->seq.store(pos + capacity + 1);
    return item;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and recover from stalls under hot paths?
- Compare hazard pointers vs epochs for this use-case.
- What happens under backpressure when the buffer is full?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess understanding of low-level synchronization, atomic operations, and memory ordering in a practical, portable way.

## Key Concepts

- Spinlock using atomic_flag
- Memory ordering: acquire on lock, release on unlock
- Exponential backoff with PAUSE to reduce contention
- Correctness test with concurrent increments

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <immintrin.h>

typedef struct { atomic_flag flag; } spinlock;

static inline void spinlock_init(spinlock* s) {
  atomic_flag_clear(&s->flag);
}

static inline void spin_lock(spinlock* s) {
  while (atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_acquire)) {
    _mm_pause();
    // small exponential backoff
    for (int b = 1; b < 1024; b <<= 1) {
      _mm_pause();
    }
  }
}

static inline void spin_unlock(spinlock* s) {
  atomic_flag_clear_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_release);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for NUMA systems to reduce cross-socket traffic?
- What are the trade-offs of using a ticket-lock vs this spinlock in high-contention workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of fundamental thread synchronization primitives, blocking behavior, and race-condition avoidance in practical concurrent data structures. It demonstrates a candidate's ability to implement classic producer-consumer patterns safely.

## Key Concepts
- **Circular buffer:** Fixed-capacity queue using modulo arithmetic for wraparound
- **Mutual exclusion:** Single mutex protecting all shared state (head, tail, count)
- **Blocking semantics:** Condition variables to suspend threads when queue is full/empty
- **Spurious wakeups:** Why `wait()` must be called with a predicate in a while loop
- **Fairness:** FIFO ordering from condition variable wakeups and serialized mutex access

## Code Example
```cpp
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <vector>

template<typename T>
class BoundedBlockingQueue {
  static constexpr size_t N = 1024;
  std::vector<T> buf_{N};
  size_t head_ = 0, tail_ = 0, count_ = 0;
  std::mutex mutex_;
  std::condition_variable not_full_, not_empty_;

public:
  void push(T item) {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    not_full_.wait(lock, [this] { return count_ < N; });
    buf_[tail_] = std::move(item);
    tail_ = (tail_ + 1) % N;
    ++count_;
    not_empty_.notify_one();
  }

  T pop() {
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    not_empty_.wait(lock, [this] { return count_ > 0; });
    T item = std::move(buf_[head_]);
    head_ = (head_ + 1) % N;
    --count_;
    not_full_.notify_one();
    return item;
  }
};
```

